---
/*
  DIY subpage 2 of 4 — How to Get Dumbphones (/diy/get-dumbphones).

  Sections, top to bottom (ticker, masthead, footer come from BaseLayout):
    1. Hero       — the M ❀ logo
    2. Heading    — cyan "* HOW TO GET DUMBPHONES" bar
    3. Body       — two blue sub-headings, a photo, and a black "submit your
                    cohort" CTA out to dumb.co
    4. Next       — link forward to the next subpage in the read-in-order set

  No "Resources" section on this page (the design doesn't have one). The blue
  sub-headings use the shared `.label` bar style (in global.css); the black
  button mirrors the DIY landing page's Lydia call-to-action.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/Hero.astro';
import SectionHeader from '../../components/SectionHeader.astro';
import NextLink from '../../components/NextLink.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="How to Get Dumbphones — Month Offline" masthead="Month Offline > DIY Guide > Dumbphones">
  <div class="sections">
    <!-- 1. Hero -->
    <Hero />

    <!-- 2. Heading -->
    <SectionHeader label="How to get dumbphones" color="cyan" />

    <!-- 3. Body -->
    <section class="card">
      <h3 class="label">Why dumb down?</h3>
      <p class="body">
        most of us have learned the hard way that we need more than just
        self-control, grayscale screens, and private resolutions to reduce our
        screen time. ppl get the most out of MO when they <u>fully abandon their
        smartphones</u> for the month.
      </p>
      <p class="body">
        why? every device has an opinion about how we should be relating,
        attending, and spending our time -- an opinion expressed in the form of
        its hardware and software design. beginning in the '90s, a couple of
        companies decided it would be a good idea to try combining telephones
        and computers into one all-purpose pocket communicator. the dumbphone
        says nah. its intentionally-limited functionality leaves room for mind
        &amp; body wandering, for fruitful friction, for glorious glitches.
      </p>

      <!-- BBC Archive clip: "1998: Can Computers and Phones Combine?" (starts at 1s). -->
      <div class="video">
        <iframe
          src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/EuF65Ohn51M?start=1"
          title="1998: Can COMPUTERS and PHONES Combine? — The Money Programme, BBC Archive"
          loading="lazy"
          allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share"
          referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
          allowfullscreen
        ></iframe>
      </div>

      <h3 class="label">What are the dumbphone deets?</h3>
      <p class="body">
        all dumbphones are welcome at Month Offline, but most ppl use the
        dumbphone 2 from dumb.co. the company was started by two of MO's
        founders <em>and</em> some of the very first participants. inspired by
        everything they learned during MO, they built a dumbphone that makes
        it easy (but not too easy) for ppl to flip off.
      </p>
      <p class="body">
        MO participants have a few options when it comes to acquiring a
        dumbphone:
      </p>
      <ul class="options">
        <li><u>borrow a refurbished dumbphone 2</u> for free (with $10 shipping) and mail it back at the end of the challenge -- recommended for ppl who are testing the waters</li>
        <li><u>get a new dumbphone 2</u> ($20 + 4-month minimum phone plan) -- recommended for ppl who want to dive right into dumb life</li>
        <li><u>bring a dumbphone they already own</u> or try an alternative from <a href="https://www.dumbphones.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dumbphone Finder</a></li>
      </ul>
      <p class="body">
        make sure participants order their phones at least 1 week before ur
        challenge begins, to allow enough time for shipping.
      </p>
      <p class="body">
        organizers will receive a free phone from dumb.co plus 3 months of
        free phone service. when u submit an <a href="https://tally.so/r/RGgD2p" target="_blank" rel="noopener">interest form</a>, we'll also share
        with u the (private) link where ppl can order a refurbished dumbphone.
      </p>

      <img
        class="card__photo"
        src="/images/dumbphones.png"
        width="1500"
        height="1000"
        alt="Participants gathered around a table setting up their dumbphones"
      />

    </section>

    <!-- 4. Ring ring -->
    <SectionHeader label="Ring ring: let's get organized" color="yellow" />
    <section class="lydia">
      <div class="lydia__top">
        <p class="body">
          ready to take the next step? submit an interest form and join the <a href="https://luma.com/calendar/cal-xd3qvSDnQeDHWEf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">next Q&amp;A session</a> to ask any questions, meet other aspiring organizers, and learn how to get dumbphones.
        </p>
        <img class="lydia__photo" src="/images/lydia.jpg" width="1200" height="1200" alt="Lydia, a Month Offline organizer" />
      </div>
      <a class="lydia__btn font-display" href="https://tally.so/r/RGgD2p">ANSWER THE CALL</a>
    </section>

    <!-- 5. Next — read-in-order, per CLAUDE.md (Get Dumbphones → Facilitate Meetups → …). -->
    <NextLink label="How to Facilitate Meetups" href="/diy/facilitate-meetups" />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* Standard body paragraph (Syne Mono). */
  .body {
    font-size: var(--size-body);
    line-height: var(--leading-body);
  }
  .body + .body {
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
  }

  /* The three acquisition options, bulleted -- same pattern as the "What
     should I bring?" list on the Facilitate Meetups page. */
  .options {
    list-style: disc;
    margin: var(--space-2) 0;
    padding-left: 1.5em;
    font-size: var(--size-body);
    line-height: var(--leading-body);
  }
  .options li + li {
    margin-top: var(--space-1);
  }

  /* Match the blue underlined link style used inside .body (global.css) --
     that rule doesn't reach into this list since it's scoped to .body. */
  .options a {
    color: var(--mo-blue);
    text-decoration: underline;
  }
  .options a:hover {
    background-color: var(--mo-blue);
    color: var(--white);
  }

  /* The photo sits between the two sub-sections inside the white card. */
  .card__photo {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    margin: var(--space-3) 0;
  }

  /* Responsive 16:9 video embed — same vertical rhythm as the photo. The
     wrapper holds the aspect ratio; the iframe fills it. */
  .video {
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    margin: var(--space-3) 0;
  }
  .video iframe {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    border: 0;
  }

</style>
